// Modo história, C2: Edimburgo, o fim da viagem (acrescentado a STORY de story.js)
STORY.push({ id: "edinburgh", level: "C2", city: "Edimburgo", flag: "🏴󠁧󠁢󠁳󠁣󠁴󠁿", about: "Um dicionário antigo, uma carta perdida e o fim da viagem… ou um novo começo.", chapters: [
  { id: "edinburgh1", icon: "🏰", title: "A cidade de pedra",
    en: ["Edinburgh in August is less a city than a fever dream. I arrived on the first day of the Festival Fringe, and the Royal Mile, the ancient spine of the Old Town, had been colonised by jugglers, fire-eaters, mime artists and students thrusting leaflets into the hands of anyone foolish enough to slow down.",
      "Above it all loomed the castle, perched on the remains of an extinct volcano with the serene indifference of something that has watched a thousand years of human folly and expects to watch a thousand more. I stood beneath it with my suitcase, utterly disoriented and, for reasons I could not articulate, profoundly happy.",
      "The city seemed to have been built in layers, each century piled carelessly on top of the last. Narrow passageways called closes plunged unexpectedly between buildings, leading down steep stone stairs to courtyards that appeared to have been forgotten by time. Every wall seemed to be keeping a secret.",
      "I found Priya in a cramped basement venue that smelled faintly of damp and spilt beer, tuning a guitar with the fierce concentration of someone about to perform surgery. When she saw me, she dropped the guitar, which Conor caught with a reflex that suggested long practice, and flung her arms around me.",
      "We hadn't seen each other for almost five years. She had cut her hair short and acquired a small tattoo of a door, a reference, she explained, to the photographs she had taken on our first day in Dublin. “You look older,” she said, “but in a good way. Less lost.”",
      "That night, the band played to a packed room of perhaps sixty people, which, by Fringe standards, constituted an overwhelming success. Aoife's voice filled the vaulted ceiling, Conor's violin wept and laughed by turns, and Priya, to my astonishment, sang a song in Portuguese.",
      "It was called “Saudade”. She had written it, she told the audience, for a friend who had taught her the word years ago in a London park. I was grateful for the darkness of the room, which concealed the state of my face rather effectively.",
      "Afterwards, we walked up to Calton Hill and watched the sun set very slowly over the rooftops, the northern summer reluctant to surrender the day. Below us, the city glowed and murmured, still restless at eleven o'clock at night.",
      "“So,” said Priya, “how long are you staying this time?” I realised that, for the first time in my life, I did not have a ready answer, and that the question no longer frightened me."],
    pt: ["Edimburgo em agosto é menos uma cidade do que um sonho febril. Cheguei no primeiro dia do Festival Fringe, e a Royal Mile, a antiga espinha dorsal da Cidade Velha, tinha sido colonizada por malabaristas, engolidores de fogo, mimos e estudantes que enfiavam folhetos nas mãos de qualquer pessoa suficientemente insensata para abrandar.",
      "Acima de tudo erguia-se o castelo, empoleirado nos restos de um vulcão extinto com a indiferença serena de algo que já assistiu a mil anos de loucura humana e conta assistir a mais mil. Fiquei por baixo dele com a mala, completamente desorientado e, por razões que não sabia explicar, profundamente feliz.",
      "A cidade parecia ter sido construída por camadas, cada século empilhado sem cuidado em cima do anterior. Passagens estreitas chamadas closes mergulhavam inesperadamente entre os edifícios, descendo por escadas íngremes de pedra até pátios que pareciam ter sido esquecidos pelo tempo. Cada parede parecia guardar um segredo.",
      "Encontrei a Priya numa cave apertada que cheirava levemente a humidade e a cerveja entornada, a afinar uma guitarra com a concentração feroz de quem está prestes a fazer uma cirurgia. Quando me viu, largou a guitarra, que o Conor apanhou com um reflexo que sugeria muita prática, e atirou-se para os meus braços.",
      "Não nos víamos há quase cinco anos. Tinha cortado o cabelo curto e feito uma pequena tatuagem de uma porta, uma referência, explicou, às fotografias que tinha tirado no nosso primeiro dia em Dublin. «Estás mais velho», disse, «mas no bom sentido. Menos perdido.»",
      "Nessa noite, a banda tocou para uma sala cheia de talvez sessenta pessoas, o que, pelos padrões do Fringe, constituía um sucesso esmagador. A voz da Aoife enchia o teto abobadado, o violino do Conor chorava e ria à vez, e a Priya, para meu espanto, cantou uma canção em português.",
      "Chamava-se «Saudade». Tinha-a escrito, disse ao público, para um amigo que lhe tinha ensinado a palavra anos antes num parque de Londres. Fiquei grato pela escuridão da sala, que escondia bastante bem o estado da minha cara.",
      "Depois, subimos a Calton Hill e vimos o sol pôr-se muito devagar sobre os telhados, o verão do norte relutante em entregar o dia. Lá em baixo, a cidade brilhava e murmurava, ainda inquieta às onze da noite.",
      "«Então», disse a Priya, «quanto tempo ficas desta vez?» Percebi que, pela primeira vez na vida, não tinha uma resposta pronta, e que a pergunta já não me assustava."],
    q: [["What does “less a city than a fever dream” suggest about Edinburgh in August?", "O que sugere «menos uma cidade do que um sonho febril» sobre Edimburgo em agosto?", ["It is chaotic, intense and almost unreal", "It is quiet and peaceful", "Many people are ill"]],
      ["What is the Royal Mile described as?", "Como é descrita a Royal Mile?", ["The ancient spine of the Old Town", "A new shopping centre", "A park near the castle"]],
      ["What does “colonised” imply about the performers?", "O que implica «colonizada» sobre os artistas?", ["They had taken over the street completely", "They came from former colonies", "They were invited by the city"]],
      ["What is the castle built on?", "Sobre o que está construído o castelo?", ["An extinct volcano", "An island", "A former harbour"]],
      ["What attitude is attributed to the castle?", "Que atitude é atribuída ao castelo?", ["Serene indifference to human folly", "Anger at the tourists", "Sadness about the past"]],
      ["How does Leo describe the way the city was built?", "Como descreve o Leo a forma como a cidade foi construída?", ["In layers, century upon century", "According to a modern plan", "Around a central square"]],
      ["What are “closes”?", "O que são «closes»?", ["Narrow passageways between buildings", "Small shops", "Old churches"]],
      ["What does the comparison with surgery tell us about Priya?", "O que nos diz a comparação com uma cirurgia sobre a Priya?", ["She takes her music very seriously", "She used to be a doctor", "She was nervous and shaking"]],
      ["What does Conor's “reflex that suggested long practice” imply?", "O que implica o «reflexo que sugeria muita prática» do Conor?", ["Priya has dropped her guitar many times before", "Conor is a professional goalkeeper", "Conor had been expecting Leo"]],
      ["What does Priya's tattoo refer to?", "A que se refere a tatuagem da Priya?", ["Her photos of doors in Dublin", "Her first concert", "The door of the flat in London"]],
      ["What does Priya mean by “Less lost”?", "O que quer a Priya dizer com «Menos perdido»?", ["Leo seems more sure of himself", "Leo no longer gets lost in cities", "Leo has lost weight"]],
      ["Why was an audience of sixty considered a great success?", "Porque era um público de sessenta considerado um grande sucesso?", ["Small venues are typical at the Fringe", "The band was very famous", "Only sixty tickets were sold in the whole festival"]],
      ["Why was Leo grateful for the darkness?", "Porque estava o Leo grato pela escuridão?", ["It hid the fact that he was crying", "He was tired", "He didn't want Priya to see him leave"]],
      ["What does “the northern summer reluctant to surrender the day” describe?", "O que descreve «o verão do norte relutante em entregar o dia»?", ["Very long daylight in northern summers", "A sudden storm", "A cold summer evening"]],
      ["What change in Leo does the final paragraph reveal?", "Que mudança no Leo revela o último parágrafo?", ["Uncertainty about the future no longer scares him", "He has decided to stay forever", "He wants to return to Sydney"]]] },
  { id: "edinburgh2", icon: "🗃️", title: "O arquivo das palavras",
    en: ["The dictionary project occupied a warren of rooms beneath the university library, accessed by a lift so old and temperamental that most of the staff preferred the stairs. The air smelled of paper, dust and the strong black tea that seemed to fuel the entire enterprise.",
      "Dr Isobel Fraser presided over this underground kingdom with a mixture of severity and dry wit that I found terrifying for approximately three weeks and indispensable thereafter. She had worked on the dictionary for thirty-one years and regarded careless etymology as something close to a moral failing.",
      "Our work consisted of tracing words back through centuries of documents, letters, court records, sermons and ballads, recording every shift in meaning, every change in spelling, every moment at which a word quietly abandoned one sense and adopted another. It was slow, meticulous and, to my surprise, utterly absorbing.",
      "On my first day, Dr Fraser set me what she called a “small exercise”: to establish the origin of the word “glamour”. I assumed it would take an hour. It took a week, and by the end of it I had learned something that delighted me out of all proportion.",
      "“Glamour”, it turns out, is a Scots alteration of “grammar”. In the Middle Ages, grammar, meaning learning in general and Latin in particular, was associated with the mysterious powers of the educated, including magic. To cast a glamour was to cast a spell. Only much later did the word come to mean elegance and allure.",
      "When I presented my findings, Dr Fraser listened in silence, then pointed out two sources I had missed and one I had misdated by half a century. I felt crushed. Then she said, “Not bad. Most people take a month and get it wrong.” From her, I later learned, this was extravagant praise.",
      "The deeper I went into the archive, the more I came to see words as living things: migrating, intermarrying, falling out of fashion, occasionally dying and, now and then, being unexpectedly resurrected. I began to suspect that every word carried the fingerprints of all the people who had ever used it.",
      "In the evenings, I walked home through the Old Town, past pubs whose names were older than most countries, and found myself mentally dissecting the signs: “close” from the Latin “clausum”, “wynd” from an Old English word for winding, “kirk” sharing a common ancestor with “church”.",
      "Priya, who had stayed in Edinburgh after the festival to record an album, declared that I had become “unbearable at dinner parties”. She was not entirely wrong. But I had not felt so certain that I was in the right place since the night I first walked into Mr O'Neill's bookshop."],
    pt: ["O projeto do dicionário ocupava um labirinto de salas por baixo da biblioteca da universidade, a que se chegava por um elevador tão velho e temperamental que a maior parte dos funcionários preferia as escadas. O ar cheirava a papel, a pó e ao chá preto forte que parecia alimentar todo o empreendimento.",
      "A Dra. Isobel Fraser governava este reino subterrâneo com uma mistura de severidade e humor seco que achei aterradora durante cerca de três semanas e indispensável a partir daí. Trabalhava no dicionário há trinta e um anos e considerava a etimologia descuidada algo próximo de uma falha moral.",
      "O nosso trabalho consistia em seguir as palavras para trás ao longo de séculos de documentos, cartas, registos de tribunal, sermões e baladas, registando cada mudança de sentido, cada alteração de grafia, cada momento em que uma palavra abandonava discretamente um sentido e adotava outro. Era lento, meticuloso e, para minha surpresa, absolutamente absorvente.",
      "No primeiro dia, a Dra. Fraser deu-me aquilo a que chamou um «pequeno exercício»: estabelecer a origem da palavra «glamour». Pensei que ia levar uma hora. Levou uma semana, e no fim tinha aprendido uma coisa que me deliciou de forma completamente desproporcionada.",
      "«Glamour», afinal, é uma alteração escocesa de «grammar». Na Idade Média, a gramática, no sentido do saber em geral e do latim em particular, estava associada aos poderes misteriosos das pessoas instruídas, incluindo a magia. Lançar um «glamour» era lançar um feitiço. Só muito mais tarde a palavra passou a significar elegância e encanto.",
      "Quando apresentei as minhas conclusões, a Dra. Fraser ouviu em silêncio, depois apontou duas fontes que me tinham escapado e uma que eu tinha datado com meio século de erro. Fiquei arrasado. Depois disse: «Nada mau. A maioria leva um mês e engana-se.» Vindo dela, soube mais tarde, era um elogio extravagante.",
      "Quanto mais fundo ia no arquivo, mais passava a ver as palavras como coisas vivas: migravam, cruzavam-se, passavam de moda, de vez em quando morriam e, uma vez por outra, ressuscitavam inesperadamente. Comecei a suspeitar de que cada palavra levava as impressões digitais de todas as pessoas que alguma vez a tinham usado.",
      "À noite, ia a pé para casa pela Cidade Velha, passando por pubs cujos nomes eram mais antigos do que a maior parte dos países, e dava por mim a dissecar mentalmente as placas: «close» do latim «clausum», «wynd» de uma palavra do inglês antigo para sinuoso, «kirk» com um antepassado comum a «church».",
      "A Priya, que tinha ficado em Edimburgo depois do festival para gravar um álbum, declarou que eu me tinha tornado «insuportável em jantares». Não estava totalmente errada. Mas eu não me sentia tão certo de estar no sítio certo desde a noite em que entrei pela primeira vez na livraria do Sr. O'Neill."],
    q: [["Where was the dictionary project located?", "Onde ficava o projeto do dicionário?", ["Beneath the university library", "In the castle", "In a modern office building"]],
      ["Why did most staff prefer the stairs?", "Porque preferia a maior parte dos funcionários as escadas?", ["The lift was old and unreliable", "The stairs were faster", "Exercise was compulsory"]],
      ["What does “a warren of rooms” suggest?", "O que sugere «um labirinto de salas»?", ["Many small, connected, confusing rooms", "One large open space", "Rooms full of rabbits"]],
      ["How did Leo's feelings about Dr Fraser change?", "Como mudaram os sentimentos do Leo em relação à Dra. Fraser?", ["From terror to finding her indispensable", "From admiration to hatred", "They never changed"]],
      ["What did Dr Fraser consider “close to a moral failing”?", "O que considerava a Dra. Fraser «próximo de uma falha moral»?", ["Careless etymology", "Being late", "Drinking too much tea"]],
      ["Which word best describes the work?", "Que palavra descreve melhor o trabalho?", ["Meticulous", "Careless", "Quick"]],
      ["How long did the “small exercise” actually take?", "Quanto tempo levou realmente o «pequeno exercício»?", ["A week", "An hour", "A month"]],
      ["What is the origin of “glamour”?", "Qual é a origem de «glamour»?", ["A Scots alteration of “grammar”", "A French word for beauty", "A Latin word for gold"]],
      ["Why was grammar associated with magic in the Middle Ages?", "Porque estava a gramática associada à magia na Idade Média?", ["Learning seemed a mysterious power of the educated", "Grammar books contained spells", "Only witches studied Latin"]],
      ["What is ironic about Dr Fraser's “Not bad”?", "O que há de irónico no «Nada mau» da Dra. Fraser?", ["For her, it was actually very high praise", "She was actually furious", "She hadn't read his work"]],
      ["How does Leo come to see words?", "Como passa o Leo a ver as palavras?", ["As living things that change and travel", "As fixed rules", "As boring historical objects"]],
      ["What does “carried the fingerprints of all the people who had ever used it” mean?", "O que significa «levava as impressões digitais de todas as pessoas que alguma vez a tinham usado»?", ["Every word is shaped by its history of use", "Old documents are dirty", "Words can be used to identify criminals"]],
      ["What does “kirk” share an ancestor with?", "Com que palavra partilha «kirk» um antepassado?", ["Church", "Castle", "Close"]],
      ["Why did Priya say Leo was “unbearable at dinner parties”?", "Porque disse a Priya que o Leo era «insuportável em jantares»?", ["He talked constantly about word origins", "He always arrived late", "He refused to eat"]],
      ["What does the final comparison with Mr O'Neill's bookshop suggest?", "O que sugere a comparação final com a livraria do Sr. O'Neill?", ["Leo feels he has found his true vocation again", "Leo wants to open a bookshop in Dublin", "Leo misses Ireland more than Scotland"]]] },
  { id: "edinburgh3", icon: "✉️", title: "A carta do marinheiro",
    en: ["In February, while sorting through a box of uncatalogued eighteenth-century correspondence that had been donated by a family in Leith, the city's old port, I came across a letter that stopped me in my tracks.",
      "It was written in a clumsy, laborious hand, on paper so fragile that I hardly dared breathe near it. The English was riddled with errors, the spelling erratic, the grammar improvised. But what made my heart race was that, here and there, scattered among the English words like stones in a field, were words in Portuguese.",
      "The letter was signed “Joam Ferreira, of Oporto, marinheiro”, and dated the fourteenth of March, 1783. It was addressed to a woman called Margaret Lindsay, and, as far as I could make out, it was a declaration of love written by a man struggling to express the most important thing in his life in a language he barely knew.",
      "“I have not the words in your tongue,” he wrote, “and in mine you have not the ears. But I think the heart do not need the grammar.” Further down, where his English evidently failed him entirely, he had written simply: “tenho saudades tuas”.",
      "I read the letter perhaps thirty times that afternoon. Dr Fraser, who rarely displayed emotion of any kind, read it twice and then sat in silence for a long moment. “Well,” she said at last, “it would appear that your countryman got here before you.”",
      "From a lexicographer's point of view, the letter was remarkable: it contained what might be one of the earliest recorded appearances of the word “saudade” in a document written in Britain, used by someone who clearly assumed his reader would not understand it and wrote it anyway.",
      "But I confess that my interest was not purely academic. Who was this sailor? Had Margaret understood him? Had she replied? Had he sailed away, as sailors invariably did in the stories, leaving behind a letter and a broken heart?",
      "The box contained no answers. There were household accounts, a few business letters and a recipe for something called “cullen skink”, which I later discovered to be a smoked fish soup. Of Joam and Margaret, there was no further trace.",
      "That night I could not sleep. I sat by the window of my flat, watching the rain trace patterns on the glass, and thought about the extraordinary loneliness of trying to love someone across a language. Then I thought about my own life, and realised that I had been doing a version of the same thing for the past seven years."],
    pt: ["Em fevereiro, ao organizar uma caixa de correspondência do século XVIII ainda por catalogar, doada por uma família de Leith, o antigo porto da cidade, encontrei uma carta que me fez parar de repente.",
      "Estava escrita com uma letra desajeitada e penosa, em papel tão frágil que mal me atrevia a respirar perto dele. O inglês estava cheio de erros, a ortografia era errática, a gramática improvisada. Mas o que me fez acelerar o coração foi que, aqui e ali, espalhadas no meio das palavras inglesas como pedras num campo, havia palavras em português.",
      "A carta estava assinada «Joam Ferreira, do Porto, marinheiro», e datada de catorze de março de 1783. Era dirigida a uma mulher chamada Margaret Lindsay e, tanto quanto consegui perceber, era uma declaração de amor escrita por um homem que lutava para exprimir a coisa mais importante da sua vida numa língua que mal conhecia.",
      "«Não tenho as palavras da tua língua», escreveu ele, «e na minha tu não tens os ouvidos. Mas penso que o coração não precisa da gramática.» Mais abaixo, onde o inglês lhe falhou por completo, tinha escrito simplesmente: «tenho saudades tuas».",
      "Li a carta talvez trinta vezes nessa tarde. A Dra. Fraser, que raramente mostrava qualquer tipo de emoção, leu-a duas vezes e depois ficou em silêncio durante um longo momento. «Bem», disse por fim, «parece que o teu conterrâneo chegou cá antes de ti.»",
      "Do ponto de vista de um lexicógrafo, a carta era notável: continha aquele que podia ser um dos primeiros registos da palavra «saudade» num documento escrito na Grã-Bretanha, usada por alguém que claramente partia do princípio de que a leitora não a ia perceber e a escreveu mesmo assim.",
      "Mas confesso que o meu interesse não era puramente académico. Quem era este marinheiro? Teria a Margaret percebido? Teria respondido? Teria ele partido, como os marinheiros invariavelmente fazem nas histórias, deixando para trás uma carta e um coração partido?",
      "A caixa não tinha respostas. Havia contas da casa, algumas cartas de negócios e uma receita de uma coisa chamada «cullen skink», que mais tarde descobri ser uma sopa de peixe fumado. De Joam e de Margaret, não havia mais rasto.",
      "Nessa noite não consegui dormir. Sentei-me à janela do meu apartamento, a ver a chuva desenhar padrões no vidro, e pensei na solidão extraordinária de tentar amar alguém através de uma língua. Depois pensei na minha própria vida, e percebi que andava a fazer uma versão da mesma coisa há sete anos."],
    q: [["Where did the box of letters come from?", "De onde vinha a caixa de cartas?", ["A family in Leith, the old port", "The castle archives", "A library in Porto"]],
      ["What does “stopped me in my tracks” mean?", "O que significa «me fez parar de repente»?", ["It surprised him so much that he stopped what he was doing", "It blocked the door", "He tripped over the box"]],
      ["How is the handwriting described?", "Como é descrita a letra?", ["Clumsy and laborious", "Elegant and fast", "Printed by a machine"]],
      ["What does the simile “like stones in a field” convey?", "O que transmite a comparação «como pedras num campo»?", ["The Portuguese words stood out, scattered among the English", "The paper was very heavy", "The letter was found outdoors"]],
      ["Who wrote the letter?", "Quem escreveu a carta?", ["A Portuguese sailor from Porto", "A Scottish merchant", "Margaret Lindsay"]],
      ["When was the letter dated?", "Qual era a data da carta?", ["1783", "1873", "1738"]],
      ["What was the nature of the letter?", "Que tipo de carta era?", ["A declaration of love", "A business contract", "A complaint about the weather"]],
      ["What does “I think the heart do not need the grammar” express?", "O que exprime «penso que o coração não precisa da gramática»?", ["Feelings can be communicated despite imperfect language", "Grammar is useless", "He refuses to learn English"]],
      ["Why did Joam write “tenho saudades tuas” in Portuguese?", "Porque escreveu o Joam «tenho saudades tuas» em português?", ["His English failed him for that feeling", "Margaret spoke Portuguese", "It was a secret code"]],
      ["What does Dr Fraser's comment “your countryman got here before you” reveal?", "O que revela o comentário da Dra. Fraser «o teu conterrâneo chegou cá antes de ti»?", ["She was moved, but expressed it with dry humour", "She was angry about the discovery", "She thought Leo had written the letter"]],
      ["Why was the letter important for the dictionary?", "Porque era a carta importante para o dicionário?", ["It might be an early record of “saudade” in Britain", "It was the oldest letter in Scotland", "It contained a new English grammar rule"]],
      ["What does Leo mean by “my interest was not purely academic”?", "O que quer o Leo dizer com «o meu interesse não era puramente académico»?", ["He was personally and emotionally involved", "He wanted to sell the letter", "He didn't care about the research"]],
      ["What cliché about sailors does Leo mention?", "Que lugar-comum sobre marinheiros menciona o Leo?", ["They always sail away leaving a broken heart", "They are always drunk", "They never learn to read"]],
      ["What is “cullen skink”?", "O que é «cullen skink»?", ["A smoked fish soup", "A type of boat", "A Scottish dance"]],
      ["What parallel does Leo draw at the end?", "Que paralelo traça o Leo no fim?", ["He too has spent years living and connecting across languages", "He too is a sailor", "He too is in love with Margaret"]]] },
  { id: "edinburgh4", icon: "🔎", title: "Ecos do Porto",
    en: ["I am not, by temperament, a detective, but the letter had lodged itself in my mind with the persistence of a splinter. For weeks, I spent my evenings and weekends searching for Joam Ferreira, with the reluctant blessing of Dr Fraser, who pretended to disapprove while quietly suggesting archives I might consult.",
      "The Scottish records yielded fragments. A ship named the Esperança had docked at Leith in the winter of 1782, carrying port wine and cork, and had been delayed for repairs until the spring. Its crew list included a “John Ferrara”, an anglicised spelling that made me laugh aloud in the silent reading room.",
      "For the Portuguese side of the story, I enlisted my mother, who, to my considerable surprise, threw herself into the task with an enthusiasm bordering on obsession. She spent three Saturdays in the municipal archive in Porto, sending me blurry photographs of baptism registers taken with her phone.",
      "It was she who found him: João Ferreira, born in 1759 in the parish of Miragaia, a few streets from the house where my grandmother had grown up. Son of a fisherman. And, crucially, no record of his death or marriage in Portugal. Wherever he had ended his life, it had not been in Porto.",
      "Back in Edinburgh, armed with this information, I searched the marriage registers of Leith. And there, in the spring of 1784, in faded ink, was the entry I had hardly dared hope for: “John Ferrara, mariner, of Oporto, and Margaret Lindsay, of this parish.”",
      "She had understood him after all. Or perhaps, more accurately, she had decided that understanding every word was not, in the end, the point.",
      "The records showed that they had remained in Leith, where Joam worked first on the docks and later, improbably, as a baker. They had six children. Their descendants were traceable, generation by generation, well into the twentieth century, when the trail disappeared into the anonymous sprawl of modern records.",
      "On a hunch, I walked down to Leith one Saturday morning and wandered through the narrow streets near the harbour. On the corner of a quiet street, I stopped dead in front of a small bakery I had passed a dozen times without paying it any attention.",
      "The faded sign above the door read: “Ferrara & Sons. Bakers since 1791.” I stood on the pavement for a very long time, the morning crowd parting around me, before I could summon the courage to go in."],
    pt: ["Não sou, por temperamento, um detetive, mas a carta tinha-se alojado na minha cabeça com a persistência de uma farpa. Durante semanas, passei as noites e os fins de semana à procura de Joam Ferreira, com a bênção relutante da Dra. Fraser, que fingia desaprovar enquanto discretamente me sugeria arquivos para consultar.",
      "Os registos escoceses deram fragmentos. Um navio chamado Esperança tinha atracado em Leith no inverno de 1782, carregado de vinho do Porto e cortiça, e tinha ficado retido para reparações até à primavera. A lista da tripulação incluía um «John Ferrara», uma grafia anglicizada que me fez rir alto na silenciosa sala de leitura.",
      "Para o lado português da história, recrutei a minha mãe, que, para minha considerável surpresa, se lançou à tarefa com um entusiasmo que roçava a obsessão. Passou três sábados no arquivo municipal do Porto, a mandar-me fotografias desfocadas de registos de batismo tiradas com o telemóvel.",
      "Foi ela que o encontrou: João Ferreira, nascido em 1759 na freguesia de Miragaia, a poucas ruas da casa onde a minha avó tinha crescido. Filho de um pescador. E, o mais importante, sem registo de morte nem de casamento em Portugal. Onde quer que tivesse acabado a vida, não tinha sido no Porto.",
      "De volta a Edimburgo, armado com esta informação, procurei nos registos de casamento de Leith. E ali, na primavera de 1784, em tinta desbotada, estava o registo que eu mal me atrevia a esperar: «John Ferrara, marinheiro, do Porto, e Margaret Lindsay, desta paróquia.»",
      "Ela tinha-o percebido, afinal. Ou talvez, mais exatamente, tivesse decidido que perceber cada palavra não era, no fim de contas, o que importava.",
      "Os registos mostravam que tinham ficado em Leith, onde o Joam trabalhou primeiro nas docas e mais tarde, improvavelmente, como padeiro. Tiveram seis filhos. Os descendentes podiam ser seguidos, geração a geração, até bem dentro do século XX, quando o rasto desaparecia na massa anónima dos registos modernos.",
      "Por intuição, desci a Leith num sábado de manhã e deambulei pelas ruas estreitas perto do porto. Na esquina de uma rua sossegada, parei de repente em frente a uma pequena padaria por onde tinha passado uma dúzia de vezes sem lhe prestar atenção.",
      "A placa desbotada por cima da porta dizia: «Ferrara & Filhos. Padeiros desde 1791.» Fiquei no passeio durante muito tempo, com a multidão da manhã a abrir-se à minha volta, antes de conseguir ganhar coragem para entrar."],
    q: [["What does the comparison with “a splinter” suggest?", "O que sugere a comparação com «uma farpa»?", ["The letter was a small but persistent, irritating thought", "Leo had hurt his hand", "The letter was made of wood"]],
      ["How did Dr Fraser really feel about Leo's search?", "O que sentia realmente a Dra. Fraser sobre a investigação do Leo?", ["She secretly supported it", "She strongly opposed it", "She didn't know about it"]],
      ["What was the ship carrying?", "O que transportava o navio?", ["Port wine and cork", "Fish and salt", "Wool and coal"]],
      ["Why did “John Ferrara” make Leo laugh?", "Porque fez «John Ferrara» o Leo rir?", ["It was an anglicised version of the Portuguese name", "It was spelled backwards", "It was the name of a famous car"]],
      ["How did Leo's mother approach the task?", "Como encarou a mãe do Leo a tarefa?", ["With enthusiasm bordering on obsession", "Reluctantly", "She refused to help"]],
      ["Where was João Ferreira born?", "Onde nasceu João Ferreira?", ["In Miragaia, Porto", "In Leith", "In Lisbon"]],
      ["Why was the absence of a death record in Portugal significant?", "Porque era significativa a ausência de um registo de morte em Portugal?", ["It suggested he had stayed abroad", "It meant he was still alive", "It meant the archive was incomplete"]],
      ["What did the marriage register prove?", "O que provava o registo de casamento?", ["Joam and Margaret had married", "Joam had returned to Portugal", "Margaret had married someone else"]],
      ["What is suggested by “understanding every word was not, in the end, the point”?", "O que se sugere com «perceber cada palavra não era, no fim de contas, o que importava»?", ["Love mattered more than perfect communication", "Margaret couldn't read", "The letter was badly translated"]],
      ["Why is Joam becoming a baker described as “improbable”?", "Porque é descrito como «improvável» o Joam ter-se tornado padeiro?", ["It was an unexpected career for a sailor", "Bakers were very rich", "He hated bread"]],
      ["How many children did they have?", "Quantos filhos tiveram?", ["Six", "Two", "Twelve"]],
      ["Why did the trail disappear in the twentieth century?", "Porque desapareceu o rasto no século XX?", ["Modern records were too vast and anonymous", "The family emigrated to Portugal", "The archive was destroyed in a fire"]],
      ["What does “On a hunch” mean?", "O que significa «por intuição»?", ["Following an intuition without proof", "Following precise instructions", "By accident"]],
      ["What is ironic about the bakery?", "O que há de irónico na padaria?", ["Leo had passed it many times without noticing", "It sold Portuguese bread", "It was closed that day"]],
      ["Why did Leo need courage to go in?", "Porque precisou o Leo de coragem para entrar?", ["The discovery was emotionally overwhelming", "The shop looked dangerous", "He had no money"]]] },
  { id: "edinburgh5", icon: "📕", title: "Um embrulho de Dublin",
    en: ["The owner of Ferrara & Sons turned out to be a large, flour-dusted man in his sixties called Robert, who listened to my somewhat incoherent explanation with growing incredulity, then disappeared into the back of the shop and returned with an old wooden box.",
      "Inside, wrapped in cloth, was a small Portuguese prayer book, its pages brittle, its margins filled with notes in two languages. On the inside cover, in the same laborious hand I knew so well, was written: “For Margaret, who learned my words.” Robert's grandmother had always said it came from “the sailor”, but nobody had known what that meant.",
      "I visited the bakery every Saturday after that. Robert gave me bread, I gave him fragments of his family's history, and we gradually reconstructed a story that neither of us could have told alone. Dr Fraser, unprompted, began drafting a paper about the letter, with my name listed first.",
      "It was in the middle of this strange, joyful spring that the parcel arrived from Dublin. I recognised the handwriting on the label immediately as that of Mr O'Neill's daughter, and something inside me went very still before I had even opened it.",
      "Mr O'Neill had died in March, peacefully, at the age of seventy-nine, in a chair in his bookshop. His daughter apologised for not writing sooner; she had only just found, among his papers, a package with my name on it and a note instructing that it be sent “to the Portuguese boy, wherever he has wandered off to”.",
      "Inside was the book of Irish stories he had given me on my first day in Dublin, which I had reluctantly returned when I left. But it was not quite the same book. Over the years, he had filled its margins with notes, small comments on each story, as though he had been continuing a conversation with me in my absence.",
      "On the last page, he had written a letter. It was short, in the economical style of a man who had spent his life surrounded by too many words. “I told you once that books are the best teachers,” it said. “I was wrong. People are. Books are just how they reach you when they are no longer in the room.”",
      "I read it sitting on the floor of my flat, and for a long time I did not move. Priya came round that evening with a bottle of wine and simply sat beside me, saying nothing, exactly as Tom had done on the beach in Sydney. I had become, it seemed, a man surrounded by people who knew when silence was the kindest language.",
      "Later that night, I placed the book of Irish stories beside Joam's prayer book on my desk, two small volumes separated by two and a half centuries, both annotated in the margins by someone trying to reach another person across the distance of language and time."],
    pt: ["O dono da Ferrara & Filhos revelou-se um homem grande, coberto de farinha, de sessenta e tal anos, chamado Robert, que ouviu a minha explicação um tanto incoerente com uma incredulidade crescente, depois desapareceu para as traseiras da loja e voltou com uma velha caixa de madeira.",
      "Lá dentro, embrulhado num pano, estava um pequeno livro de orações português, de páginas quebradiças, com as margens cheias de notas em duas línguas. No interior da capa, com a mesma letra penosa que eu conhecia tão bem, estava escrito: «Para a Margaret, que aprendeu as minhas palavras.» A avó do Robert sempre tinha dito que vinha «do marinheiro», mas ninguém sabia o que isso queria dizer.",
      "Passei a ir à padaria todos os sábados. O Robert dava-me pão, eu dava-lhe fragmentos da história da família dele, e aos poucos reconstruímos uma história que nenhum de nós teria conseguido contar sozinho. A Dra. Fraser, sem ninguém lhe pedir, começou a escrever um artigo sobre a carta, com o meu nome em primeiro lugar.",
      "Foi a meio dessa primavera estranha e feliz que chegou o embrulho de Dublin. Reconheci logo a letra na etiqueta como sendo da filha do Sr. O'Neill, e qualquer coisa dentro de mim ficou muito quieta antes mesmo de o abrir.",
      "O Sr. O'Neill tinha morrido em março, tranquilamente, aos setenta e nove anos, numa cadeira da sua livraria. A filha pedia desculpa por não ter escrito antes; só agora tinha encontrado, no meio dos papéis dele, um embrulho com o meu nome e uma nota a pedir que fosse enviado «ao rapaz português, para onde quer que ele se tenha ido meter».",
      "Lá dentro estava o livro de histórias irlandesas que ele me tinha dado no primeiro dia em Dublin, e que eu tinha devolvido com pena quando me fui embora. Mas não era bem o mesmo livro. Ao longo dos anos, ele tinha enchido as margens de notas, pequenos comentários a cada história, como se tivesse continuado uma conversa comigo na minha ausência.",
      "Na última página, tinha escrito uma carta. Era curta, no estilo económico de um homem que tinha passado a vida rodeado de palavras a mais. «Disse-te uma vez que os livros são os melhores professores», dizia. «Enganei-me. As pessoas é que são. Os livros são só a maneira de elas chegarem até ti quando já não estão na sala.»",
      "Li-a sentado no chão do meu apartamento e, durante muito tempo, não me mexi. A Priya apareceu nessa noite com uma garrafa de vinho e limitou-se a sentar-se ao meu lado, sem dizer nada, exatamente como o Tom tinha feito na praia em Sydney. Tinha-me tornado, ao que parece, um homem rodeado de pessoas que sabiam quando o silêncio era a língua mais gentil.",
      "Mais tarde nessa noite, pus o livro de histórias irlandesas ao lado do livro de orações do Joam, em cima da secretária: dois pequenos volumes separados por dois séculos e meio, ambos anotados nas margens por alguém que tentava chegar a outra pessoa através da distância da língua e do tempo."],
    q: [["How did Robert first react to Leo's story?", "Como reagiu o Robert à história do Leo ao princípio?", ["With growing incredulity", "With anger", "With complete indifference"]],
      ["What was in the wooden box?", "O que havia na caixa de madeira?", ["A Portuguese prayer book", "The original love letter", "Old bread recipes"]],
      ["What does the inscription “For Margaret, who learned my words” reveal?", "O que revela a dedicatória «Para a Margaret, que aprendeu as minhas palavras»?", ["Margaret had learned Portuguese", "Margaret had written the book", "Joam had never married her"]],
      ["What did Robert's grandmother say about the book?", "O que dizia a avó do Robert sobre o livro?", ["That it came from “the sailor”", "That it was stolen", "That it was worthless"]],
      ["What does “unprompted” tell us about Dr Fraser?", "O que nos diz «sem ninguém lhe pedir» sobre a Dra. Fraser?", ["She acted on her own initiative", "She was forced to write the paper", "She didn't want to help"]],
      ["Why is it significant that Leo's name was listed first?", "Porque é significativo que o nome do Leo aparecesse em primeiro lugar?", ["It gave him the main credit for the discovery", "It was in alphabetical order", "Dr Fraser forgot her own name"]],
      ["How did Leo react before opening the parcel?", "Como reagiu o Leo antes de abrir o embrulho?", ["He sensed bad news and became very still", "He was excited and impatient", "He threw it away"]],
      ["How did Mr O'Neill die?", "Como morreu o Sr. O'Neill?", ["Peacefully, in a chair in his bookshop", "In a car accident", "In hospital after a long illness"]],
      ["What tone does Mr O'Neill's note “wherever he has wandered off to” have?", "Que tom tem a nota do Sr. O'Neill «para onde quer que ele se tenha ido meter»?", ["Affectionate and gently teasing", "Angry and bitter", "Formal and cold"]],
      ["How was the book different from before?", "Em que era o livro diferente de antes?", ["Its margins were filled with notes for Leo", "It had a new cover", "Some pages were missing"]],
      ["What does “the economical style of a man who had spent his life surrounded by too many words” suggest?", "O que sugere «o estilo económico de um homem que tinha passado a vida rodeado de palavras a mais»?", ["He wrote briefly and precisely", "He didn't have much money", "He never read books"]],
      ["What did Mr O'Neill change his mind about?", "Sobre o que mudou o Sr. O'Neill de opinião?", ["People, not books, are the best teachers", "Books are better than people", "Leo should not have travelled"]],
      ["How did Priya comfort Leo?", "Como consolou a Priya o Leo?", ["She sat beside him in silence", "She gave him a long speech", "She took him to a concert"]],
      ["What does “silence was the kindest language” mean?", "O que significa «o silêncio era a língua mais gentil»?", ["Sometimes saying nothing comforts more than words", "Leo stopped speaking for weeks", "Silence is a language studied in linguistics"]],
      ["What connects the two books on Leo's desk?", "O que liga os dois livros na secretária do Leo?", ["Both were annotated to reach someone across language and time", "Both were written in Irish", "Both were bought in the same bookshop"]]] },
  { id: "edinburgh6", icon: "🏠", title: "Saudade",
    en: ["The paper Dr Fraser and I wrote about Joam's letter was published that autumn and, to our mutual astonishment, attracted a degree of attention wholly disproportionate to its academic significance. A newspaper in Lisbon ran the story under the headline “The Sailor Who Taught Scotland Saudade”, and Robert's bakery acquired a small but devoted stream of Portuguese tourists.",
      "Dr Fraser offered me a permanent position on the dictionary. It was, by any rational measure, the job I had spent my entire adult life drifting towards. I asked for a week to think about it, which surprised her, and surprised me rather more.",
      "I spent that week walking. Up Arthur's Seat in a gale, along the Water of Leith, through the cemeteries of the Old Town, where the gravestones bore names that had travelled from every corner of Europe. And everywhere I went, I found myself thinking, absurdly and insistently, of a street in Miragaia.",
      "I had spent seven years learning to belong elsewhere. I had become, in some measure, a Londoner, a Dubliner, a New Yorker, a Torontonian, a Sydneysider and, latterly, an honorary resident of Leith. What I had never done was return to Porto as the person all those places had made me.",
      "I turned down the job. Dr Fraser received the news with a long, inscrutable silence and then, to my bewilderment, embraced me, briefly and stiffly, like someone performing an unfamiliar ritual. “You'll write to the dictionary,” she said. “With citations. Properly dated.”",
      "A year later, on a bright morning in June, a small shop opened on a corner in Miragaia, two streets from where João Ferreira was baptised. Half bookshop, half café, it sold second-hand books in six languages and the worst coffee in Porto, in deliberate homage to a diner in Brooklyn. Above the door, a sign read: “Saudade — Livros e Línguas”.",
      "On the opening day, the shop was absurdly overcrowded. Mrs Brown came from London, older and slower but with the same red umbrella, bringing a photograph of Biscuit, who had died peacefully at a great age, presumably on someone's suitcase. Priya played in the corner. Jamal had sent forty photographs of faces from every city I had lived in, and they covered an entire wall.",
      "On the counter, under glass, lay two books: a book of Irish stories with notes in the margins, and a small jar of Australian sand. Above them, framed, hung a copy of Joam's letter, open at the line that had started it all: “I think the heart do not need the grammar.”",
      "That evening, after everyone had gone, my sister Inês, now eighteen and alarmingly grown-up, sat on the counter swinging her legs and asked whether I would teach her English properly. I looked around the empty shop, at the wall of faces and the shelves of books in six languages, and said that I would. Then I took a notebook from the drawer, opened it at the first page, and wrote: “My name is Leo. I am from Porto, in Portugal…”"],
    pt: ["O artigo que eu e a Dra. Fraser escrevemos sobre a carta do Joam foi publicado nesse outono e, para espanto de ambos, atraiu uma atenção totalmente desproporcionada em relação à sua importância académica. Um jornal de Lisboa publicou a história com o título «O marinheiro que ensinou a saudade à Escócia», e a padaria do Robert passou a receber um pequeno mas fiel fluxo de turistas portugueses.",
      "A Dra. Fraser ofereceu-me um lugar permanente no dicionário. Era, por qualquer critério racional, o emprego para o qual eu tinha andado à deriva toda a minha vida adulta. Pedi uma semana para pensar, o que a surpreendeu a ela, e me surpreendeu bastante mais a mim.",
      "Passei essa semana a caminhar. Subi o Arthur's Seat no meio de um vendaval, andei ao longo do Water of Leith, pelos cemitérios da Cidade Velha, onde as lápides tinham nomes vindos de todos os cantos da Europa. E em todo o lado dava por mim a pensar, de forma absurda e insistente, numa rua de Miragaia.",
      "Tinha passado sete anos a aprender a pertencer a outros sítios. Tinha-me tornado, de certa forma, londrino, dublinense, nova-iorquino, torontino, sydneysiano e, mais recentemente, residente honorário de Leith. O que nunca tinha feito era voltar ao Porto como a pessoa que todos esses sítios tinham feito de mim.",
      "Recusei o emprego. A Dra. Fraser recebeu a notícia com um silêncio longo e indecifrável e depois, para minha perplexidade, abraçou-me, de forma breve e rígida, como quem executa um ritual desconhecido. «Vais escrever ao dicionário», disse. «Com citações. Devidamente datadas.»",
      "Um ano depois, numa manhã luminosa de junho, abriu uma pequena loja numa esquina de Miragaia, a duas ruas de onde João Ferreira foi batizado. Metade livraria, metade café, vendia livros em segunda mão em seis línguas e o pior café do Porto, numa homenagem deliberada a um restaurante de Brooklyn. Por cima da porta, uma placa dizia: «Saudade — Livros e Línguas».",
      "No dia da inauguração, a loja estava absurdamente cheia. A Sra. Brown veio de Londres, mais velha e mais lenta mas com o mesmo guarda-chuva vermelho, e trouxe uma fotografia do Biscuit, que tinha morrido tranquilamente com uma idade avançada, presumivelmente em cima da mala de alguém. A Priya tocou a um canto. O Jamal tinha mandado quarenta fotografias de caras de todas as cidades onde eu tinha vivido, e cobriam uma parede inteira.",
      "No balcão, debaixo de um vidro, estavam dois objetos: um livro de histórias irlandesas com notas nas margens e um frasquinho de areia australiana. Por cima, emoldurada, estava uma cópia da carta do Joam, aberta na linha que tinha começado tudo: «Penso que o coração não precisa da gramática.»",
      "Nessa noite, depois de toda a gente se ir embora, a minha irmã Inês, agora com dezoito anos e assustadoramente crescida, sentou-se no balcão a balançar as pernas e perguntou-me se eu lhe ensinava inglês como deve ser. Olhei à volta da loja vazia, para a parede de caras e as prateleiras de livros em seis línguas, e disse que sim. Depois tirei um caderno da gaveta, abri-o na primeira página e escrevi: «My name is Leo. I am from Porto, in Portugal…»"],
    q: [["Why does Leo describe the attention as “wholly disproportionate”?", "Porque descreve o Leo a atenção como «totalmente desproporcionada»?", ["It was far greater than the paper's academic importance justified", "Nobody read the paper", "The paper was very long"]],
      ["What was the Lisbon newspaper's headline?", "Qual era o título do jornal de Lisboa?", ["“The Sailor Who Taught Scotland Saudade”", "“A Portuguese Baker in Scotland”", "“The Lost Letter of Leith”"]],
      ["Why did Leo's request for a week surprise even himself?", "Porque surpreendeu o pedido de uma semana o próprio Leo?", ["It was the job he had always seemed to be heading for", "He had already accepted another job", "He didn't like Dr Fraser"]],
      ["What does “drifting towards” imply about Leo's career?", "O que implica «andado à deriva» sobre a carreira do Leo?", ["It developed without a clear plan", "It was carefully planned from the start", "It was going badly"]],
      ["What kept coming into Leo's mind during his walks?", "O que lhe vinha constantemente à cabeça durante as caminhadas?", ["A street in Miragaia, in Porto", "The beach in Sydney", "The diner in Brooklyn"]],
      ["What does the detail about gravestones from all over Europe suggest?", "O que sugere o pormenor das lápides de toda a Europa?", ["Edinburgh has always been a city of migrants", "Many tourists die in Edinburgh", "The cemeteries are very old"]],
      ["What had Leo never done?", "O que nunca tinha feito o Leo?", ["Return to Porto as the person his travels had made him", "Live in a big city", "Work in a bookshop"]],
      ["How did Dr Fraser react to Leo's decision?", "Como reagiu a Dra. Fraser à decisão do Leo?", ["With silence, then an awkward embrace", "With anger and shouting", "With complete indifference"]],
      ["What does “like someone performing an unfamiliar ritual” reveal about Dr Fraser?", "O que revela «como quem executa um ritual desconhecido» sobre a Dra. Fraser?", ["She rarely shows affection physically", "She is very religious", "She hugs people all the time"]],
      ["Why does Dr Fraser insist on “citations, properly dated”?", "Porque insiste a Dra. Fraser em «citações, devidamente datadas»?", ["It is her characteristic way of showing she wants to stay in touch", "She doesn't trust Leo", "She wants him to pay a fee"]],
      ["Why is the shop's location meaningful?", "Porque é significativa a localização da loja?", ["It is near where João Ferreira was baptised", "It is next to Leo's university", "It is the cheapest street in Porto"]],
      ["Why does the shop serve the worst coffee in Porto?", "Porque serve a loja o pior café do Porto?", ["As a deliberate homage to Marcus's diner", "Because Leo can't make coffee", "To save money"]],
      ["What does the phrase “presumably on someone's suitcase” refer back to?", "A que se refere a expressão «presumivelmente em cima da mala de alguém»?", ["Biscuit sleeping on Leo's suitcase in London", "Mrs Brown's travel habits", "Priya's guitar case"]],
      ["What do the objects under the glass represent?", "O que representam os objetos debaixo do vidro?", ["The people and lessons from Leo's journey", "Things for sale", "Gifts from his family"]],
      ["Why is the ending of the story circular?", "Porque é que o final da história é circular?", ["Leo writes the same words that opened the very first chapter", "Leo goes back to London", "Inês leaves Porto as Leo did"]]] },
] });
